# Line endings that scripts actually REQUIRE to run.
#
# core.autocrlf is per-user local config — it does not travel with the repo,
# so a fresh clone or a different machine gets no guarantee. These rules do.
#
# Why this file exists: deploy_on_windows.bat ended up with pure LF endings
# (0 CRLF across 32 lines). cmd.exe mis-reads line boundaries in that state
# and drops the leading characters of every command — FIREBASE_CLI_EXPERIMENTS
# executed as 'BASE_CLI_EXPERIMENTS', Taking as 'king'. The deploy did nothing
# and still exited 0, so it looked like it had succeeded.

# Batch files are parsed by cmd.exe and MUST be CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts MUST be LF — a CRLF .sh fails with "bad interpreter: \r",
# and scripts/snapshot.sh (the pre-deploy backup) is invoked through Git's
# sh.exe on Windows, so it is exposed to exactly this.
*.sh text eol=lf
